wb-cli (1.8.0) stable; urgency=medium

  Closes out the serial-plugin reorganization started in 1.6.0:
  bus-level slave_id / baud writes are now first-class commands,
  ``send-modbus`` joins raw ``send`` as a higher-level wrapper, and
  the ``modbus-fw`` plugin folds in as ``serial wb-fw`` — everything
  that touches an RS-485 port lives under one plugin.

  ## Added — bus-level writes

  * ``serial wb-set-slave-id <current_id> <new_id> --port <path>
    [--sn HEX] [UART overrides]`` — change a device's slave_id.
    Default uses standard Modbus FC6 reg 128 (works on WB devices and
    every device that follows the wiki's Common Modbus Registers).
    With ``--sn`` the write goes via WB Fast Modbus, safe under
    physical address collisions (two devices answering at the same
    slave_id). NOT for non-Modbus devices on the same RS-485 (e.g.
    Энергомера meters with their own IEC-style protocol, DOOYA blinds
    with a proprietary one), and not for third-party Modbus devices
    that change their slave_id through a different register or a
    vendor-specific procedure — check the datasheet.
  * ``serial wb-set-baud <slave_id> <new_baud> --port <path>
    [UART overrides]`` — change a device's baud rate via FC6 reg 110.
    Same WB-only caveat as above.

  Both share the bus-fixup helpers ``add-devices`` already uses; the
  helpers moved into ``wb_cli/lib/serial_bus.py`` so the two flows can
  reuse them cleanly.

  ## Added — Modbus-protocol send

  * ``serial send-modbus --port <path> --slave <id> --fc <code>
    --reg <addr> [--value N | --count N]`` — convenience wrapper over
    ``serial send``: builds the Modbus PDU + CRC for you and computes
    the expected response size per function code. Supported function
    codes: 3 (read holding), 4 (read input), 6 (write single).
    Raw ``serial send`` stays as the escape hatch for arbitrary
    protocols and unusual Fast Modbus frames.

  ## Moved — modbus-fw → serial wb-fw

  * ``wb-cli modbus-fw <action>`` → ``wb-cli serial wb-fw <action>``.
    Same logic and arguments, same RPC (wb-device-manager fw-update);
    just lives under the ``serial`` plugin now since firmware update
    is one more thing you do over an RS-485 port. The top-level
    ``modbus-fw`` plugin is removed.
  * ``wb-cli serial wb-fw update --background`` schedules its job as
    ``wb-cli --json serial wb-fw update --wait …``; existing
    ``modbus-fw-update-*`` units finish on the old binary path and
    are unaffected.

  ## Migration

  * ``modbus-fw check 4 …``           → ``serial wb-fw check 4 …``
  * ``modbus-fw update 4 …``          → ``serial wb-fw update 4 …``
  * ``modbus-fw restore 4 …``         → ``serial wb-fw restore 4 …``
  * ``serial send … --msg '<addr> 06 00 80 …' --add-modbus-crc``
                                       → ``serial wb-set-slave-id``
  * ``serial send … --msg '<addr> 06 00 6E …' --add-modbus-crc``
                                       → ``serial wb-set-baud``

 -- Alexander Degtyarev <a.degtyarev@wirenboard.com>  Wed, 13 May 2026 13:00:00 +0000

wb-cli (1.7.0) stable; urgency=medium

  Breaking minor — first batch of the ``serial`` plugin tidy-up
  promised in 1.6.0. Renames four legacy subcommands to two with
  cleaner semantics; no functional regressions.

  ## Removed (renamed)
  * ``serial devices`` and ``serial device-info`` → folded into one
    ``serial config [<slave_id>] [--port <path>]``. Without an id —
    compact list, optionally port-filtered (was ``devices``). With
    an id — full dict of one device (was ``device-info``). One name
    for the config layer, easier to find from help.
  * ``serial device-params`` (read) and ``serial device-set`` (write)
    → folded into one ``serial fw-params <slave_id> [k=v ...] [--force]``.
    Empty positional list → read (was ``device-params``); non-empty
    pairs → write (was ``device-set``, dropped the ``--set k=v --set
    k=v`` flag-repeat for plain positional ``k=v k=v``).

  ## Changed semantics — fw-params write
  * Default ``fw-params <id> k=v`` writes **through the config**
    (confed Load → patch device dict → confed Save → driver reload).
    Persistent: the value survives a wb-mqtt-serial restart because
    the config is the source of truth. Previous ``device-set`` went
    straight through ``device/Set`` RPC and was therefore one-shot:
    next driver restart would replay the old value from the config.
  * ``--force`` on write keeps the old one-shot behaviour (direct
    ``wb-mqtt-serial/device/Set`` RPC, no config write) — explicit
    opt-in for tests / probes where you don't want to persist.
  * ``--force`` on read still bypasses the driver's parameter cache
    (unchanged from ``device-params --force``).

  ## Tests
  * Rewrote 11 existing ``test_serial`` tests onto the new names and
    the two write paths (``via=config`` / ``via=device``). Existing
    ``test_modbus_device_info_*`` and ``test_modbus_devices_*`` in
    ``test_commands`` renamed to ``test_serial_config_*``. 287 tests
    total, lint 10.00/10.
  * skills/wb-serial: every ``serial devices`` / ``device-info`` /
    ``device-params`` / ``device-set`` reference rewritten.

  Still scheduled separately:
  * ``serial wb-set-slave-id`` / ``wb-set-baud`` (bus-level via WB
    Common Modbus Registers / Fast Modbus by SN)
  * ``serial send-modbus`` (Modbus-protocol wrapper over ``port/Load``)
  * Plugin ``modbus-fw`` → subcommand ``serial wb-fw``

 -- Alexander Degtyarev <a.degtyarev@wirenboard.com>  Wed, 13 May 2026 16:35:49 +0400

