wb-cli (0.3.1) stable; urgency=medium

  * NEW: `wb-cli rules enable <name>` — inverse of `disable`. The pair was
    missing in 0.3.0.
  * NEW: `wb-cli modbus devices` — dumps every configured device from
    /etc/wb-mqtt-serial.conf with the effective UART parameters (port-level
    defaults overridden by per-device fields).
  * `modbus-fw check` and `modbus-fw update` now accept the slave_id as
    optional. Without it they walk every device from the serial config:
      wb-cli modbus-fw check                   # bulk check against config
      wb-cli modbus-fw update --all            # bulk update everything
                                                 with can_update=true
                                                 (--all is required to
                                                 acknowledge the bulk write)
    Each row in bulk check carries either the version info or an `error`
    string when the RPC fails for that device.
  * lib/serial_conf.py — extracted parsing of /etc/wb-mqtt-serial.conf so
    both `modbus` and `modbus-fw` share it. Plugins live under wb_cli/lib/
    when they have logic worth sharing.
  * `rules disable` / `rules enable` now do a plain `mv` of the rule file
    instead of going through wbrules' Editor RPC — the editor rejects any
    save/rename whose name doesn't end in .js, so 0.3.0's implementation
    failed for both directions. wb-rules watches /etc/wb-rules/ via inotify,
    so the rename takes effect on its own. Both commands now also refuse to
    overwrite an existing target (new RULES_TARGET_EXISTS error) and report
    RULES_NOT_FOUND when the source is missing.
  * `rules load` unwraps wb-rules' current envelope shape
    (`{"content": "<source>", "enabled": true}`) and returns just the source.
    0.3.0 returned the whole dict and broke any caller that piped the result
    back into `rules save`.
  * `modbus templates` got a human-mode renderer (was falling through to
    the JSON-fallback table).
  * `modbus-fw check` bulk now shows the per-device `error` column in the
    human table when any row has one — previously the JSON envelope kept it,
    but the rendered table dropped it and ERROR rows were unexplained.
  * RpcClient attaches an upgrade hint when a server returns an ImportError
    (typical symptom of an out-of-date wb-device-manager / wb-rules / etc.
    on the controller's system Python). The hint tells the user which
    package to `apt-get install`.

 -- Alexander Degtyarev <a.degtyarev@wirenboard.com>  Tue, 12 May 2026 00:30:00 +0000

wb-cli (0.3.0) stable; urgency=medium

  * `modbus scan` now mirrors the three buttons in the web UI:
      (default)     «Поиск устройств» — WB Fast Modbus pass; on a modern
                    bus this is what finds everything.
      --slow        «Начать медленное сканирование» — classic Modbus poll
                    over every UART combo. Use when the default misses
                    devices (old firmware without Fast Modbus, non-default
                    UART). Raise --timeout (default 60s isn't enough).
      --bootloader  «Поиск устройств в режиме загрузчика» — for devices
                    stuck after a failed `modbus-fw update`.
    Always passes `preserve_old_results=false` so the result is fresh;
    waits a short stable window after progress=100 so the trickle of
    last-second device updates makes it into the output. `--port` goes
    into the RPC instead of being a post-filter. Output is a proper
    aligned table with a header and includes `scan_type`. On timeout the
    partial result is returned with `completed=false` + `progress`, not
    discarded.

  * NEW plugin `modbus-fw` — firmware update over RS-485 (the web UI's
    "Check / Update" buttons):
      wb-cli modbus-fw check <slave_id> --port <path>
      wb-cli modbus-fw update <slave_id> --port <path> [--wait]
      wb-cli modbus-fw restore <slave_id> --port <path> [--wait]
      wb-cli modbus-fw clear-error <slave_id> --port <path>
      wb-cli modbus-fw watch                  # live progress for everything
      wb-cli modbus-fw state                  # one-shot status
    `--wait` blocks until the device finishes/errors, drawing a stderr
    progress bar; otherwise the call returns once wb-device-manager
    accepts the request and the user can `modbus-fw watch` later.

  * lib/rpc: server error messages from `mqtt-rpc-client` go to *stdout*,
    not stderr — fall back to stdout when stderr is empty so the caller
    actually sees what the RPC server said.

  * BREAKING — `devices` plugin is gone. Everything now lives under one verb:
      wb-cli dev                                # every <device>/<control>
                                                 with value/type/readonly/error
      wb-cli dev <device>                       # controls of one device
      wb-cli dev <device>/<control>             # read one
      wb-cli dev <device>/<control> <value>     # write one
      wb-cli dev --all                          # include system_* devices
    The wb-rules-style `<device>/<control>` address handles spaces in
    control names — quote them. List output is a compact three-column
    table (`address`, `value`, `type/flags`) so it fits in a stock 120-col
    terminal; the `error` column appears only when at least one row has
    one.

  * BREAKING — human is now the default output mode everywhere, including
    pipes (so `wb-cli dev | grep` works the unix way). LLM agents and
    scripts must pass `--json` (or set `WB_CLI_OUTPUT=json`) — already
    documented in the wiren-board skill.
  * Subscribe is correct under wildcard+spaces. mosquitto_sub was
    full-buffering stdout under a pipe, and Python's BufferedReader was
    eating subsequent lines, so meta/+ for "Input 0" returned partial
    results (and `devices set` could silently skip the readonly check).
    We now run mosquitto_sub through stdbuf -oL and read the raw fd via
    os.read().
  * Help screens rewritten in plain English with concrete examples,
    including how to quote control names with spaces and values with
    spaces (`wb-cli dev 'wb-mdm3_5/Channel 1 Dimming Level' 30`).
  * Error code cleanup (post-0.2 audit):
      AUDIT_BASELINE_NOT_FOUND  -> SNAPSHOT_BASELINE_NOT_FOUND
      AUDIT_INVALID_SNAPSHOT    -> SNAPSHOT_INVALID
      HISTORY_INVALID_PERIOD    -> HISTORY_INVALID_CHANNEL
      MODBUS_ADD_NO_DEVICES     -> split into MODBUS_ADD_INVALID_JSON
                                              MODBUS_ADD_EMPTY
                                              MODBUS_ADD_PORT_NOT_FOUND
      modbus device-info uses    MODBUS_DEVICE_NOT_FOUND (was DEVICES_*)
      shell.py: TIMEOUT/INTERNAL renamed to SHELL_TIMEOUT/SHELL_COMMAND_FAILED
      cli.py:   INTERNAL -> CLI_INTERNAL
  * `snapshot save` now returns {"ok": true} like every other side-effect
    command; timestamp label is UTC.
  * `mqtt list` envelope renamed: `topics` -> `messages` (consistent with
    `mqtt read`'s `{topic, payload}` shape).
  * `history`: `point_count` -> `count`; timestamp filter no longer leaks
    a stray `{"timestamp": {}}` when neither --from nor --to is set.
  * `rules disable` renames .js -> .js.disabled (used to overwrite the
    file with empty content, losing the source).
  * Dead code removed: per-subparser `-q/--quiet` (unused; only the
    top-level CliContext.quiet was ever read), `args.human=False` in
    every test Namespace.

 -- Alexander Degtyarev <a.degtyarev@wirenboard.com>  Mon, 11 May 2026 20:30:00 +0000

wb-cli (0.2.1) stable; urgency=medium

  * MQTT subscribe is now ~10x faster. Previously `-W <timeout>` kept the
    connection open for the full timeout even after every retained message
    had arrived (so `devices list` blocked for 5 seconds, `devices inventory`
    for 15). We now stream mosquitto_sub via a Popen and exit after a 300 ms
    idle window once the first message has been received. On the test
    controller: inventory dropped from ~15 s to ~1 s, list from ~5 s to ~1 s.
  * `devices list` now hides `system_*` wrappers (per-network connections,
    cloud-agent state, system_time) by default. Pass `--all` to keep them.

